SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR deeply concerned by renewed violence displacing thousands in North Kivu, DR Congo","publisher":"UNHCR","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/unhcr-deeply-concerned-renewed-violence-displacing-thousands-north-kivu-dr","claim":"UNHCR said more than 72,000 people had been displaced in recent days by fighting in North Kivu, with the broader escalation since November 2021 displacing at least 170,000 civilians.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN humanitarian briefing with a concrete displacement count.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact early-2022 crisis baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited humanitarian emergency rather than a ruler-attributed external-war decision.","contrary_evidence":["Humanitarian severity alone does not distinguish Tshisekedi's own contribution from inherited conflict dynamics."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.10"]}

8. Mid-2022 civilian suffering and attacks.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR gravely concerned by death toll of displaced in DR Congo's east","publisher":"UNHCR","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/unhcr-gravely-concerned-death-toll-displaced-dr-congos-east","claim":"UNHCR warned that eastern DRC civilians were suffering a growing death toll and noted simultaneous armed attacks in Ituri that killed 11 people and burned or looted 250 homes.","locator":"briefing note body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN humanitarian reporting with concrete casualty and destruction figures.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact July 2022 escalation period.","ruler_attribution":"This is the harm environment Tshisekedi was trying to manage, not a direct action by him.","contrary_evidence":["UNHCR reports harm and displacement, but not command responsibility for the violence."],"lenses":["2B.4","2B.10"]}

9. Defence-council response, hardening, and continued peace-process commitment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Communiqué officiel du Conseil Supérieur de la Défense présidé ce samedi 29 octobre 2022 par le Chef de l'État, Félix Tshisekedi","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-10-29","url":"https://presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/communique_officiel_du_conseil_superieur_de_la_defense_preside_ce_samedi_29_octobre_2022_par_le_chef_de_letat_felix_tshisekedi","claim":"The defense council said the M23 offensive was backed by the Rwandan army, ordered the government to expel the Rwandan ambassador, increase force posture, send humanitarian aid, and reaffirmed engagement in the Nairobi and Luanda peace processes while warning against hate speech targeting rwandophone communities.","locator":"numbered recommendations in the communiqué","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government communiqué from a high-level security meeting chaired by the president.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-2022 security response.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Tshisekedi's chairing of the council and the decisions announced under his authority.","contrary_evidence":["The communiqué is accusatory and self-justifying; the Rwanda-support claim remained contested in the broader information environment."],"lenses":["2B.5","2B.6","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

10. National address on the eastern crisis.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Adresse à la nation du Président de la République, Félix Tshisekedi suite à la détérioration de la situation sécuritaire à l’est du pays","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/adresse_a_la_nation_du_president_de_la_republique_felix_tshisekedi_suite_a_la_deterioration_de_la_situation_securitaire_a_lest_du_pays","claim":"Tshisekedi told the nation that the M23 was occupying territory in North Kivu with support from Rwanda, said more than 200,000 Congolese had fled, and instructed the government to organize assistance for displaced people.","locator":"opening paragraphs of the address","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary ruler speech with explicit crisis framing and government instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact November 2022 crisis point.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler rhetoric and directive in the context of the eastern war.","contrary_evidence":["The address is strongly self-justifying and its external-attribution claims are disputed outside the presidency."],"lenses":["2B.6","2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}

11. Luanda mini-summit ceasefire and withdrawal decision.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Le mini-sommet de Luanda impose au m23/Rwanda un cessez-le-feu et le retrait des zones occupées en RDC","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/le_mini_sommet_de_luanda_impose_au_m23rwanda_un_cessez_le_feu_et_le_retrait_des_zones_occupees_en_rdc","claim":"The Luanda mini-summit, attended by Tshisekedi and regional leaders, imposed a ceasefire on M23, required withdrawal from occupied areas, linked disarmament and cantonment to the EAC regional force and MONUSCO, and demanded an end to external support for M23.","locator":"article body and quoted communiqué summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential report of a signed regional peace decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 23-24 November 2022 peace decision.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Tshisekedi as summit participant and signatory to the peace framework.","contrary_evidence":["This is a summit outcome, not proof that the ceasefire held or that M23 complied."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}

12. Independent Reuters confirmation of the Luanda ceasefire.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Accord sur un cessez-le-feu dans l'est de la RDC","publisher":"Reuters via Boursorama","publication_date":"2022-11-23","url":"https://www.boursorama.com/actualite-economique/actualites/accord-sur-un-cessez-le-feu-dans-l-est-de-la-rdc-abd0b00c6a2bc6e3cee87abde7569e40","claim":"Reuters reported that African leaders announced a ceasefire in eastern DRC starting Friday and said East African regional forces would intervene against M23 if the truce was not respected.","locator":"lead paragraph and Reuters dateline","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent wire reporting syndicated by a major outlet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-November 2022 ceasefire announcement.","ruler_attribution":"Independent confirmation of the regional peace deal Tshisekedi joined.","contrary_evidence":["The report still leaves compliance and effectiveness unresolved."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

13. AU-UN support for the EAC/Nairobi/Luanda track.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Joint Communiqué: Sixth (6th) African Union - United Nations Annual Conference, Addis Ababa, 1st December 2022","publisher":"African Union","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://au.int/sw/node/42447","claim":"The AU and UN renewed support for the EAC-led peace process and its facilitator, welcomed the Luanda mini-summit final communiqué, and called for its full implementation in eastern DRC.","locator":"paragraph 13 on the Democratic Republic of Congo","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary AU-UN joint communiqué summarizing the formal international peace track.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact year-end 2022 peace-process context.","ruler_attribution":"Regional/international endorsement of the peace path Tshisekedi pursued, not a direct presidential act by itself.","contrary_evidence":["International endorsement does not prove implementation or lasting stability on the ground."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across presidency releases, AU/EAC pages, UN humanitarian and peacekeeping material, Reuters syndications, and rights-monitoring reports.
- I kept only sources that added distinct value on authority, de-escalation, civilian harm, peace-process steps, or end-state change.
- I rejected repeated coverage of the same June and November peace meetings when the later or independent source added no new factual claim.
- I rejected broad conflict summaries, generic DRC background pages, and later 2023/2024 follow-ups unless they were needed only to describe a 2022 institutional framework.
- I rejected any source that would have duplicated the same peace-process claim without adding a different claim, locator, or source type.
- I did not use the client matrix, and I did not re-fetch the local structured datasets already provided in the prior.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still no direct evidence of Tshisekedi personally controlling battlefield conduct, proxy behavior, or FARDC accountability in a way that would resolve all 2B.4/2B.5/2B.9 questions cleanly.
- The record is strong on peace-process participation but weaker on implementation results, especially whether regional agreements materially reduced violence by year-end 2022.
- The most disputed factual point remains Rwanda’s role; the presidency treats it as established, while independent material often frames it as allegation, concern, or contested attribution.
- More search would help mainly on two questions: whether there were verifiable compliance mechanisms for the Luanda/Nairobi agreements, and whether Congolese forces were credibly restrained from abuses during the 2022 campaign.

chapter_notes.3B:
**Reused Evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["3B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.5","3B.9","3B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0010"],"lenses":["3B.9","3B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0015"],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0016"],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0017"],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}

**New Evidence**
- UN monthly rights reporting gives the cleanest state/non-state split for May 2022: state agents were responsible for 188 violations and at least 21 extrajudicial executions, while armed groups caused most of the rest. It is strong baseline evidence for domestic violence and attribution.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La situation des droits de l’homme dans le pays en mai 2022","publisher":"Les Nations Unies en RD Congo","publication_date":"2022-07-05","url":"https://drcongo.un.org/fr/188998-la-situation-des-droits-de-l%E2%80%99homme-dans-le-pays-en-mai-2022","claim":"In May 2022, BCNUDH documented 551 human-rights violations, 188 attributed to state agents, including at least 21 extrajudicial executions, while armed groups were responsible for 363 violations and at least 290 summary executions.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN country-office monthly bulletin with quantified findings and attribution split.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact May 2022 snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"State-agent abuses are attributable to institutions under Tshisekedi's chain of command, though many incidents were carried out by provincial/security actors.","contrary_evidence":["Armed groups still caused more violations overall, so the state was not the only or even main source of violence."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.6","3B.10"]}

- Amnesty's state-of-siege report is the clearest evidence that emergency rule was degrading civic space rather than protecting it.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo: Justice and freedoms under siege in North-Kivu and Ituri","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/afr62/5495/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said the state of siege had failed to improve security and instead enabled military authorities to undermine expression, assembly, and justice through blanket protest bans, military-court trials of civilians, and arbitrary detentions.","locator":"executive summary and background","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed rights-monitoring report grounded in contemporary field evidence and legal analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 continuation of the state of siege.","ruler_attribution":"The state of siege was Tshisekedi's declared policy; day-to-day abuses were often by military administrators, but the policy and its extensions were presidential-government choices.","contrary_evidence":["The report focuses on the eastern provinces under state of siege, not the whole country, and some abuses were carried out by military authorities rather than direct presidential orders."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}

- HRW's annual 2022 summary captures the pattern of protest repression, child protection failure, and the weak remedial response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"HRW said authorities banned demonstrations and used excessive force; in April police killed five people during peaceful protests in Goma, Butembo, and Beni, and Tshisekedi later apologized to children involved only after appointing the police commander in charge of the roundup as mayor of Goma.","locator":"Freedom of Expression, Peaceful Assembly, and Media section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent annual rights review with specific incidents and presidential response details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Summarizes 2022 conduct and end-of-year consequences.","ruler_attribution":"The killings were by police/provincial security forces; the apology and appointment are ruler-level decisions that show a mixed, weak accountability response.","contrary_evidence":["The source mixes national and provincial actors, so direct presidential responsibility is partial rather than exclusive."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- The U.S. State Department report is useful for the mixed picture: some prosecutions, persistent impunity, and continuing abuse by security forces.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089109.html","claim":"The report says the government took some steps to identify, investigate, prosecute, and punish officials who committed abuses, but impunity remained common, particularly at higher levels.","locator":"executive summary and security-force accountability sections","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Mirror of a U.S. government country report with explicit accountability assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the 2022 period and its immediate aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"This is ruler-relevant because it describes the administration's remedial record and the limits of enforcement under Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":["The report also documents continued arbitrary arrests, protest repression, and abuse by security forces."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

- Early-year press repression shows the pattern was not isolated to the eastern provinces.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC authorities detain 3 journalists for over a week in insult case","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/drc-authorities-detain-3-journalists-for-over-a-week-in-insult-case/","claim":"Police detained three Radio Mwana Mboka journalists in Bumba for more than a week over an alleged insult broadcast about a public official.","locator":"first four paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with named victims, dates, and custody details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact May 2022 incident.","ruler_attribution":"The arrests were carried out by provincial police and prosecutors, but they reflect the broader national climate of repression under Tshisekedi's government.","contrary_evidence":["The case is local and prosecutorial rather than a direct presidential order."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

- Another prolonged detention case shows protest coverage itself was risky.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Two Congolese journalists detained for 5 months after covering a protest","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/05/two-congolese-journalists-detained-for-5-months-after-covering-a-protest/","claim":"Patrick Lola and Christian Bofaya were detained without charge for five months after covering a protest in Mbandaka.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with clear detention chronology and attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 pattern of continued detention.","ruler_attribution":"Local police/prosecutor action, but it is relevant to the national civic-space climate under Tshisekedi.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not prove national-level direction from the presidency."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

- The capital-city protest case is important because it combines protest policing and press intimidation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC police detain, beat 3 journalists covering protest","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-09-02","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/09/drc-police-detain-beat-3-journalists-covering-protest/amp/","claim":"Police arrested, detained, and physically abused three journalists while they covered an opposition protest in Kinshasa on August 29, 2022.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with named journalists, date, and police action.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-August 2022 incident.","ruler_attribution":"Carried out by national police rather than the president personally, but still relevant to state restraint under his administration.","contrary_evidence":["The specific abuse was by police officers in the field, not directly ordered by the president in the article."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

- Military-intelligence abuse is especially relevant because it points closer to the national chain of command.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DRC military detains, beats journalist Christophe Katembo Muthahinga","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/10/drc-military-detains-beats-journalist-christophe-katembo-muthahinga/","claim":"Four military intelligence agents arrested and beat journalist Christophe Katembo Muthahinga in North Kivu over his traffic coverage.","locator":"first five paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with direct victim testimony and agent identification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact October 2022 incident.","ruler_attribution":"This is the closest 2022 press-abuse item to national-security command, because the perpetrators were military-intelligence agents, though the article still does not show a direct presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["The incident is still a local operational abuse, not a documented order from the presidency."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8","3B.9"]}

- The child-protection record in the east shows the scale of civilian vulnerability Tshisekedi inherited and struggled to control.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Les enfants sous attaque dans l'escalade du conflit à l'est de la RDC","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-06-25","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/communiques-presse/enfants-sous-attaque-escalade-du-conflit-est-rdc","claim":"UNICEF said recent fighting in North Kivu killed four children, displaced more than 158,000 people since late March, left about 41,000 displaced children out of school, and separated 652 children from parents or guardians.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN child-protection bulletin with concrete displacement and child-victim counts.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact June 2022 escalation period.","ruler_attribution":"These are not acts by Tshisekedi, but they are the domestic protection burden his government faced.","contrary_evidence":["The source documents harm, not direct ruler action."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}

- The November UNICEF alert shows the crisis worsening and children becoming more exposed to GBV and disease.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF: Mass displacements in eastern DRC pose a deadly threat to children and young people","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-11-07","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/unicef-mass-displacements-eastern-drc-pose-deadly-threat-children-and-young-people","claim":"UNICEF warned that fighting in eastern DRC had displaced about 100,000 people in 10 days, left nearly 190 children separated from their families, and put children at heightened risk of gender-based violence and cholera.","locator":"lead paragraphs and quote block","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN emergency response release with specific figures and child-protection risks.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact November 2022 crisis point.","ruler_attribution":"Again, this is inherited domestic insecurity rather than direct presidential abuse, but it reflects the end-state safety environment.","contrary_evidence":["It is humanitarian-response material, not proof of presidential responsibility for the displacement itself."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}

- The GBV forum gives a ruler-period policy-response marker, though it is not proof of success.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The national forum of traditional and religious leaders to end GBV","publisher":"UNFPA Democratic Republic of Congo","publication_date":"2022-11-11","url":"https://drc.unfpa.org/en/news/national-forum-traditional-and-religious-leaders-end-gbv","claim":"At the November 2022 forum, UNFPA said 42% of GBV cases recorded in 2021 in the DRC concerned people under 18.","locator":"forum report paragraph on Diene Keita's remarks","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN agency report tied to a 2022 national forum with a specific GBV statistic.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Very close to the target year and directly relevant to the 2022 protection environment.","ruler_attribution":"This is a state-wide protection context marker rather than a direct presidential act, but it frames the scale of vulnerable-group harm Tshisekedi needed to address.","contrary_evidence":["The statistic is from 2021 and the forum itself does not show implementation success."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}

- The end-of-year GBV tally is useful as a limited positive indicator that the government and partners were at least mobilizing a response.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"16 Jours d’activisme contre les violences basées sur le genre : UNFPA RDC au coeur de la lutte contre les VBG","publisher":"UNFPA Democratic Republic of Congo","publication_date":"2022-12-28","url":"https://drc.unfpa.org/fr/news/16-jours-dactivisme-contre-les-violences-bas%C3%A9es","claim":"UNFPA said more than 97,000 GBV cases had been reported and taken care of in the DRC in 2022, while also stressing that the situation remained worrying and that government engagement was not enough on its own.","locator":"middle section of the article on the 16-days campaign","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UN agency campaign report with a concrete 2022 caseload figure, but it is programmatic rather than an audit of state performance.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact end-of-2022 state of play.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the response environment under Tshisekedi's government, but not a ruler-specific effectiveness finding.","contrary_evidence":["The source simultaneously says the situation remained alarming, so the caseload is not a success metric."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}

- Tshisekedi's government did move on press-law reform, which is the main favorable civic-space item I found.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RDC: le gouvernement adopte le texte modifiant la loi sur l’exercice de la liberté de la presse","publisher":"Actualite.cd","publication_date":"2022-10-15","url":"https://actualite.cd/index.php/2022/10/15/rdc-le-gouvernement-adopte-le-texte-modifiant-la-loi-sur-lexercice-de-la-liberte-de-la","claim":"The government adopted a draft law amending the 1996 press law to extend it to freedom of information and online media in line with the constitution.","locator":"article body, lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Local reporting on a concrete cabinet decision with direct policy content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact October 2022 policy choice.","ruler_attribution":"This is a government-level reform under Tshisekedi's administration, though the article does not prove final enactment.","contrary_evidence":["It is a draft-law adoption, not proof that the reform was passed, implemented, or improved safety on the ground."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8"]}

- The president's own council record on the Kwamouth communal conflict shows direct crisis-management attention.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Compte rendu de la soixante-douzième réunion du Conseil des Ministres","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-10-08","url":"https://www.presidence.cd/actualite-detail/actualite/compte_rendu_de_la_soixante-douzieme_reunion_du_conseil_des_ministres","claim":"Tshisekedi told the Council that the Teke-Yaka conflict in Kwamouth had reached alarming proportions with deaths and material losses and that humanitarian care for displaced people was required.","locator":"communication item on the Kwamouth situation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential council record with direct ruler communication and instructions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact October 2022 domestic-crisis management.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential attention and instructions, but the violence itself was local communal conflict rather than state violence.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a response record, not evidence that the underlying communal violence was brought under control."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- The Kishishe response is the strongest direct accountability item in the dossier.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Compte rendu de la soixante-dix-neuvième réunion du Conseil des Ministres","publisher":"Présidence RDC","publication_date":"2022-12-04","url":"https://www.presidence.cd/conseil-de-ministres/compte_rendu_de_la_soixante_dix_neuvieme_reunion_du_conseil_des_ministres","claim":"After the Kishishe massacre, Tshisekedi ordered three days of national mourning and instructed the justice minister to open an internal investigation and pursue an international investigation without delay.","locator":"communication item on Kishishe and the follow-on instructions","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential council record with explicit orders and accountability steps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact December 2022 crisis response.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential response and command to investigate, though the massacre itself was committed by M23/Rwandan-backed forces rather than the state.","contrary_evidence":["This is a response after mass violence, not proof that preventive domestic protection was effective beforehand."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.9","3B.10"]}

- HRW's Buhene piece is a useful reminder that state-agent violence and impunity were not limited to protests.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DR Congo: No Inquiry into Police Role in Ethnic Killings","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/23/dr-congo-no-inquiry-police-role-ethnic-killings","claim":"HRW said police were implicated in the Buhene killings and looting, but a year later there had been no meaningful inquiry or discipline; it also noted that Tshisekedi declared martial law and later appointed the police commander in charge of the attacks as deputy territory administrator.","locator":"main body paragraphs on inquiry failure and appointment","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-documented rights report with concrete attribution and accountability failure details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Still directly relevant in 2022 because the lack of inquiry and appointment both fall in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"The killings were local police/state-agent conduct; the appointment is a presidential decision that weakens the accountability signal.","contrary_evidence":["Some violence predated the presidential appointment and originated in local ethnic conflict dynamics."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.9"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across UN/OHCHR, Amnesty, HRW, CPJ, UNICEF, UNFPA, presidency releases, local-language reporting, and U.S. State Department materials.
- I kept sources that added distinct value on state-agent violence, protest repression, journalist intimidation, emergency-rule abuse, vulnerable-group harm, and concrete remedial steps.
- I rejected repeated coverage of the same journalist-abuse pattern when it added no new factual claim beyond a different date or place.
- I rejected purely international-conflict material that belonged in Chapter 2B unless it had a direct domestic safety consequence, and I skipped sources that only repeated the eastern war narrative without a domestic-protection angle.
- I rejected later 2023-2025 summaries unless they were only used to describe a 2022 policy or baseline; I avoided them when a contemporaneous 2022 source existed.
- I did not use the client matrix, and I did not re-fetch the structured local-prior datasets.

**Remaining Gaps**
- I still do not have direct, ruler-attributed evidence for a presidential order to arrest or abuse specific journalists, protesters, or activists; most incidents are carried out by police, military, or local administrators.
- The record is strong on repression and weak on implementation of remedies. The clearest positive items are the press-law draft, the Kishishe inquiry order, and some prosecutions cited in the State Department report, but I do not have a clean audit showing durable improvement.
- Child protection and GBV evidence is strong on harm and service demand, but weaker on whether Tshisekedi's administration measurably improved protection in 2022.
- The most valuable next search, if you want it, would be one focused pass on implementation evidence: prosecutions, disciplinary actions, or formal investigative reports on the 2022 protest deaths and police/military abuses.

chapter_notes.4B:
The 2022 dossier is mixed: Tshisekedi’s government kept formal electoral competition alive and did some legal/administrative work around the 2023 election, but the year was also marked by contested control of electoral machinery, repeated protest suppression under the state of siege, and sustained pressure on journalists and opposition figures.

**Reused evidence**
